[[File:Ojodake.jpg|thumb|Ojodake on Mt. Aso where ''Takeiwatatsu-no-Mikoto'' practised his archery.]]
According to an interview with several priests at Aso shrine, the legend of Takeiwatatsu-no-Mikoto and the formation of Aso City, is as follows:

Thousands of years ago, the crater now known as Aso city was said to be filled with water and was thereby a huge lake surrounded by the outer rim of the {{ill|Aso Caldera|lt=caldera|ja|Aso Caldera|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Aso Caldera|qq=}}. Takeiwatatsu-no-Mikoto kicked in the western side of the {{ill|Aso Caldera|lt=caldera|ja|Aso Caldera|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Aso Caldera|qq=}}<ref name="Takano-2006" /> at what is now known as {{ill|Tateno Station (Kumamoto)|lt=Tateno|en|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tateno Station (Kumamoto)|qq=}}, {{ill|Minamiaso, Kumamoto|lt=Minami-Aso|en|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Minamiaso, Kumamoto|qq=}}. He fell from the effort and was unable to stand for a while hence the name "Tateno" meaning "''unable to stand up''."As the water drained away forming rivers on the outside of the valley, a huge catfish (''{{ill|Namazu (Japanese mythology)|lt=Namazu|en|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Namazu (Japanese mythology)|qq=}})'' appeared. The catfish was settled at the bottom of the lake where some stories say it was asked to leave by Takeiwatatsu-no-Mikoto. The catfish went to Namazu, {{ill|Kashima, Kumamoto|lt=Kashima|en|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kashima, Kumamoto|qq=}} where Namazu-sha Shrine was established to pay homage to the soul of the catfish as the spirit of the lake. Now the shrine is in the precincts of the Kokuzo Shrine.<ref name="Aso GeoPark Geosite Map-2014">{{Cite news|date=2014|title=The Deity of the Volcano Geosite|work=Aso GeoPark Geosite Map|publisher=Aso Local Development Design Centre Foundation|type=Guide Map|url=http://www.aso-geopark.jp/}}</ref>

